.TH "PERFORMANCE.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Performance.toJSON \- Performance: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Performance\f[R]
interface returns a JSON\-serializable plain object representing the
\f[CR]Performance\f[R] object.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]Performance\f[R] object is encountered during JSON serialization.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A JSON\-serializable plain object containing the following properties:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]timeOrigin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]timing\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]navigation\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]interactionCount\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
When the returned object is passed to \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], the
\f[CR]timing\f[R] and \f[CR]navigation\f[R] properties are further
serialized using \f[CR]PerformanceTiming.toJSON()\f[R] and
\f[CR]PerformanceNavigation.toJSON()\f[R], respectively.
.PP
The returned object doesn\(cqt contain the \f[CR]eventCounts\f[R]
property because \f[CR]EventCounts\f[R] doesn\(cqt provide a
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] operation.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] In a window context, the returned object includes
\f[CR]interactionCount\f[R] and the deprecated
\f[CR]performance.timing\f[R] and \f[CR]performance.navigation\f[R]
properties.
These properties are not available in workers.
To get a JSON\-serializable plain object representing a
\f[CR]PerformanceNavigationTiming\f[R] entry, call
\f[CR]PerformanceNavigationTiming.toJSON()\f[R] instead.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly on \f[CR]performance\f[R] returns a
plain object.
Its properties have the same values as the corresponding properties of
the original object.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] json = performance.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// A plain object\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
console.log(json.timeOrigin); \f[I]// Same value as performance.timeOrigin\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
In this example, the \f[CR]Performance\f[R] object is serialized using
\f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method
automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(performance));
.EE
.PP
This would log a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtimeOrigin\(dq: 1668077531367.4,
  \(dqtiming\(dq: {
    \(dqconnectStart\(dq: 1668077531372,
    \(dqnavigationStart\(dq: 1668077531367,
    \(dqsecureConnectionStart\(dq: 0,
    \(dqfetchStart\(dq: 1668077531372,
    \(dqdomContentLoadedEventStart\(dq: 1668077531580,
    \(dqresponseStart\(dq: 1668077531372,
    \(dqdomInteractive\(dq: 1668077531524,
    \(dqdomainLookupEnd\(dq: 1668077531372,
    \(dqresponseEnd\(dq: 1668077531500,
    \(dqredirectStart\(dq: 0,
    \(dqrequestStart\(dq: 1668077531372,
    \(dqunloadEventEnd\(dq: 0,
    \(dqunloadEventStart\(dq: 0,
    \(dqdomLoading\(dq: 1668077531512,
    \(dqdomComplete\(dq: 1668077531585,
    \(dqdomainLookupStart\(dq: 1668077531372,
    \(dqloadEventStart\(dq: 1668077531585,
    \(dqdomContentLoadedEventEnd\(dq: 1668077531580,
    \(dqloadEventEnd\(dq: 1668077531585,
    \(dqredirectEnd\(dq: 0,
    \(dqconnectEnd\(dq: 1668077531372
  },
  \(dqnavigation\(dq: {
    \(dqtype\(dq: 0,
    \(dqredirectCount\(dq: 0
  },
  \(dqinteractionCount\(dq: 0
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
